@packages/compiler/src/proposal-workspace.sigil import { SigilProposalWorkspace }
@packages/compiler/src/evaluation.sigil import { SigilCompilationEvaluation }
@packages/compiler/src/report-protocol.sigil import { SigilCompilationReportProtocol }
@packages/compiler/src/event-protocol.sigil import { SigilCompilationEventProtocol }
@packages/compiler/src/session-store.sigil import { SigilCompilationSessionStore }

component SigilCompilationSession {
  goal {
    Own reusable proposal-compilation session identity, sequencing, serialization,
    refresh, expiry, latest-report retention, and cleanup coordination.
  }

  interface {
    CompilationSessionLifecycle {
      evaluate(CompilationProposal proposal, SessionEvaluationOptions?) streams
      CompilationEvent and returns CompilationReport for the next completed proposal
      generation, rejects cancellation with COMPILER_CANCELLED, or rejects with a
      stable proposal, caller/session, workspace-condition, busy, or host-failure
      code

      refresh replaces the immutable proposal base epoch, clears generation history,
      and returns CompilationSessionRefreshResult or rejects with a stable busy,
      COMPILER_SESSION_EXPIRED, or workspace failure

      close releases the session and proposal workspace or rejects with a stable
      busy, workspace-condition COMPILER_WORKSPACE_OWNERSHIP_UNVERIFIED, or
      host-failure code
    }

    SessionEvaluationOptions {
      cancellationSignal is an optional caller-owned signal observed without giving
      the caller a session lease or proposal-workspace capability
    }

    CompilationSessionRefreshResult {
      contains the committed new baseEpoch and base fingerprint and contains no
      CompilationReport or CompilationEvent terminal outcome
    }
  }
}

expand SigilCompilationSession {
  state {
    CompilationSessionLifecycle {
      A compilation session is active, evaluating, refreshing, closing, closed,
      expired, cleanup-failed, or manual-recovery-required.
    }
  }

  logic {
    CompilationSessionLifecycle {
      Bind the selected target, profile, focus, capability contract, and first
      immutable base epoch at creation.

      Persist and restore session lifecycle through SigilCompilationSessionStore so
      each CLI operation may execute in an independent process.

      For evaluate, establish a compatible writable CompilationEvent stream before
      opening the session record or reading proposal-workspace state. Stream
      establishment failure rejects with COMPILER_FAILED and performs no session
      access.

      After opening the verified record and SessionLease, invoke
      SigilProposalWorkspace restore while the lease remains held. Own the returned
      façade and restored ProposalWorkspaceAccess, release that access before apply,
      refresh, or close, and never ask SigilCompilationSessionStore to manage the
      proposal-workspace lifecycle.

      Delegate accepted proposals to SigilCompilationEvaluation through the selected
      focus and its complete dependency closure using ProposalWorkspaceAccess. Treat
      its CompilationEvaluationResult and nonterminal progress as internal inputs;
      SigilCompilationEvaluation never settles the session operation.

      Pass coreFilesystem only to SigilCore and pass agentRoot only through the
      adapter's enforced read-only sandbox; never receive or forward the façade's
      writable capability.

      Record the generation allocated and returned by SigilProposalWorkspace after
      successful materialization.

      Hold each ProposalWorkspaceAccess while the current serialized operation lease
      remains acquired. Invoke its idempotent release before committing or releasing
      the session lease after creation or after evaluation completes, fails, or is
      cancelled, and never retain it for a later operation.

      Serialize evaluate, refresh, and close; reject a competing operation with
      COMPILER_SESSION_BUSY.

      Supply SigilCompilationReportProtocol constructReport with the internal
      evaluation result and prior completed report selected for the same base epoch,
      then retain the authoritative report it returns without deriving diagnostic
      lifecycle or report status in the session.

      On cancellation, terminate delegated work, emit one cancelled terminal event
      carrying COMPILER_CANCELLED and exact session, baseEpoch, generation, and
      proposal-fingerprint identity when transport remains writable, and return no
      CompilationReport. Failure to write that required terminal event supersedes
      cancellation with COMPILER_FAILED.

      Observe SessionEvaluationOptions cancellation until evaluation returns an
      accepted internal result and durable SessionRecord commit begins. Cancellation
      before commit begins preserves the generation, commits no report, and rejects
      with COMPILER_CANCELLED. Once commit begins, ignore later cancellation and let
      commit plus terminal settlement determine the outcome.

      Use SigilCompilationEventProtocol for ordered progress and terminal event
      framing. Proposal, caller/session, workspace-condition, busy, and dependency
      host failures reject with their exact stable code and use that code in a failed
      terminal event when transport is writable. Event protocol, transport, or
      compiler-orchestration failure rejects with COMPILER_FAILED. Because evaluate
      establishes its stream first, later session or workspace rejection always
      attempts its required failed terminal event; terminal-write failure supersedes
      it with COMPILER_FAILED.

      On refresh, close SigilProposalWorkspace and create its replacement. Increment
      baseEpoch and clear generation and diagnostic history only after replacement
      creation succeeds.

      If closing the old façade during refresh rejects with an ordinary workspace
      state or host failure, do not create a replacement, retain the old baseEpoch
      and history as inaccessible evidence, enter cleanup-failed, and permit only
      close to retry cleanup. Ownership-unverified instead enters permanent manual-
      recovery-required behavior.

      If replacement creation fails after the old façade closes, retain the old
      baseEpoch and history as inaccessible evidence, enter cleanup-failed, reject
      evaluate and refresh with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE, and permit only close to
      release the replacement façade before the caller creates a new session.

      Schedule ordinary expiry or abandoned-session cleanup only when no operation
      holds the session lease. As the explicit exception, commit-failure and
      interrupted-commit recovery retain the failing operation's lease while the
      session invokes verified SigilProposalWorkspace close and session-store
      removal, then release the lease after cleanup rejects or removal succeeds.

      After accepted evaluate produces its report, release ProposalWorkspaceAccess,
      stage generation, latest-report, lifecycle, and expiry changes, and commit the
      SessionRecord while retaining the lease. Only after successful commit release
      the lease, emit a completed terminal event, and return its authoritative
      CompilationReport.

      After successful refresh replacement, release ProposalWorkspaceAccess, stage
      the new baseEpoch, base fingerprint, cleared generation and report history,
      lifecycle, and expiry, and commit the SessionRecord while retaining the lease.
      Then release the lease and return CompilationSessionRefreshResult without
      constructing a report or emitting a CompilationEvent terminal event.

      SigilCompilationSession exclusively requests evaluate completed terminal
      settlement after durable commit. Failure writing that terminal event rejects
      with COMPILER_FAILED and returns no report without undoing the already durable
      session state.

      If evaluate durable commit fails, expose no completed event or
      CompilationReport, reject with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE, and emit a
      failed event when transport remains writable. If refresh durable commit fails,
      reject with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE without emitting a
      CompilationEvent. For either operation, use the still-live façade and retained
      lease to close the proposal workspace and remove verified session storage; the
      session becomes permanently unusable. Ownership-unverified during cleanup takes
      precedence and requires manual recovery, while any other cleanup failure
      preserves evidence and releases the lease without permitting later evaluation.

      When restore detects that verified proposal materialization advanced beyond the
      preceding durable SessionRecord, classify it as process interruption after
      materialization and before commit. Expose no ProposalWorkspaceAccess, retain
      the restoration façade and lease only for verified close and storage removal,
      reject with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE, and permanently abandon the session.
      Ownership-unverified requires manual recovery; other cleanup failure preserves
      evidence without permitting reuse.

      Set expiresAt to the configured session TTL after successful session creation.
      Reset it to the configured session TTL after every accepted evaluate or refresh
      settles, including cancellation or proposal, workspace-condition, or host
      rejection.

      At or beyond expiresAt, expire before accepting another operation. If an
      operation acquired its lease before expiresAt, let it settle, reset expiresAt,
      release its access and lease, and only then reconsider expiry.

      Expiry closes SigilProposalWorkspace. Successful cleanup enters expired;
      cleanup failure enters cleanup-failed and permits only close to retry.

      On COMPILER_WORKSPACE_OWNERSHIP_UNVERIFIED, persist lifecycle state as manual-
      recovery-required through the still-verified session store, release the lease,
      and expose only read-only status identifying required operator recovery.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    CompilationSessionLifecycle {
      Generate sessionIdentity as a UUID version 4 using the host cryptographic
      random source and pass its canonical lowercase representation to
      SigilProposalWorkspace.

      Cancellation preserves the materialized generation and active session, leaves
      the latest completed report unchanged, and never records a cancelled report as
      latest.

      A completed evaluation report may replace the latest completed report. Invalid
      proposal, caller/session failure, workspace condition, host failure, and
      cancellation reject without replacing it.

      Consume ProposalWorkspaceFailure category and code without reconstructing or
      overriding SigilProposalWorkspace failure classification.

      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_OWNERSHIP_UNVERIFIED makes the session permanently unusable,
      enters manual-recovery-required, and permits no evaluate, refresh, close retry,
      or automatic deletion. Read-only status may report the terminal condition.

      Refresh creates a new baseEpoch even when the recomputed content-derived base
      fingerprint remains equal.

      Refresh replacement failure after terminal old-workspace close never restores
      the prior workspace or active session.

      Refresh never creates a replacement after old-workspace close rejection.

      Busy, invalid-session, and read-only status requests do not reset expiresAt.

      Evaluate and refresh in expired reject with COMPILER_SESSION_EXPIRED. Close in
      expired succeeds idempotently and preserves the expired terminal state.

      Cleanup failure during expiry permanently blocks evaluation and refresh in that
      session; later close may finish cleanup but never restores the session.

      Green is evidence for one exact proposal generation and never grants human
      approval.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    ProposalCompilationSessions {
      Decision: Evaluate exact proposal generations through reusable sessions backed
      by an isolated SigilProposalWorkspace.

      Scope: Governs proposal evaluation, base-epoch sequencing, and latest completed
      report retention; excludes report identity, diagnostic lifecycle, CLI
      transport, and Sigil-change approval.

      Trade-offs: The session owns base-epoch identity, serialization, refresh,
      expiry, and cleanup coordination.

      Discarded alternatives: One-shot workspaces and prompt-only proposals were
      rejected as resource-heavy or incompatible with direct tool inspection.

      Consequences: Iterations reuse one copied base while reports retain exact
      generation identity.
    }

    ProposalSessionExpiry {
      Decision: Expire an inactive proposal session after the configured session TTL
      and retry abandoned-session cleanup through SigilProposalWorkspace during later
      session management.

      Scope: Governs local proposal sessions; excludes active evaluations and
      ordinary compilation history.

      Assumptions: The workspace chooses a retention interval appropriate for its
      design-conversation duration and temporary-content exposure.

      Trade-offs: Resumability retains confidential temporary contents and disk usage
      longer.

      Discarded alternatives: Process-lifetime and indefinite retention were rejected
      as too short or unsafe.

      Consequences: Sessions survive normal pauses without becoming permanent hidden
      caches.

      Revisit when: Usage or security policy requires a different interval.
    }

    RefreshReplacementFailure {
      Decision: Treat failure to close the old proposal workspace or to create its
      replacement as terminal for evaluation in that session and preserve the old
      epoch and history only as inaccessible evidence.

      Scope: Governs refresh failure while closing the old workspace or creating its
      replacement.

      Trade-offs: A failed refresh may require a new session even though the durable
      record retains the prior identity and history.

      Design issues addressed: A closed proposal-workspace façade cannot be reopened
      or safely presented as active after replacement creation fails.

      Consequences: Evaluate and refresh remain blocked, while close may finish
      verified cleanup of any failed replacement state.
    }

    DurableSessionSettlement {
      Decision: Commit durable session state and release operation capabilities
      before exposing a completed event or returning its CompilationReport.

      Scope: Governs evaluation commit and completed settlement ordering; excludes
      refresh return settlement, report construction, and event-envelope
      compatibility.

      Trade-offs: A durable commit failure terminates the session and requires a new
      one even when evaluation already produced an in-memory report.

      Design issues addressed: A caller must never observe successful completion
      while another process restores stale session state, and process interruption
      must have one deterministic recovery outcome.

      Discarded alternatives: Emitting completion before persistence and retaining a
      possibly divergent session after commit failure were rejected.

      Consequences: Completed settlement is durable; failed persistence exposes no
      successful report and triggers verified cleanup. A later opener detecting
      materialization newer than the record abandons rather than repairs or reuses
      that session. Cancellation cannot interrupt an in-progress durable commit.
    }

    ManualRecoveryBoundary {
      Decision: Represent unverifiable proposal-workspace ownership as a distinct
      persisted terminal session state with no automatic cleanup operation.

      Scope: Governs session behavior after COMPILER_WORKSPACE_OWNERSHIP_UNVERIFIED;
      excludes operator-directed filesystem recovery outside compiler authority.

      Design issues addressed: Cleanup-failed permits verified close retry, while
      unverifiable ownership must not authorize another automatic filesystem action.

      Discarded alternatives: Treating ownership failure as retryable cleanup was
      rejected because the compiler can no longer prove its deletion authority.

      Consequences: Read-only status can explain abandonment, while recovery remains
      an explicit operating-system user action.
    }

    SessionRefreshSettlement {
      Decision: Settle refresh as a committed base-identity change rather than a
      compilation report or event-stream run.

      Scope: Governs successful refresh return and durability ordering; excludes
      proposal evaluation and terminal CompilationEvent settlement.

      Design issues addressed: Refresh changes the frozen base but performs no
      evaluation from which an authoritative CompilationReport could be constructed.

      Consequences: Callers receive the new committed base epoch and fingerprint;
      later evaluate creates the first report for that epoch.
    }
  }

  cases {
    CompilationSessionLifecycle {
      Successive generations reuse one base epoch without external compilation
      history.

      Refresh increments baseEpoch and resets history; its base fingerprint changes
      only when selected evidence changes. It commits before returning the new epoch
      and fingerprint and emits no CompilationReport or terminal CompilationEvent.

      Replacement creation failure after old-workspace close leaves baseEpoch and
      history unchanged but inaccessible, enters cleanup-failed, blocks evaluate and
      refresh, and permits only close. Successful close requires a new session.

      Old-workspace close failure during refresh creates no replacement, preserves
      inaccessible prior evidence, and permits only close retry; ownership-unverified
      requires manual recovery.

      Competing evaluate, refresh, or close rejects with COMPILER_SESSION_BUSY.

      Cancellation preserves the generation. Expiry during evaluation defers cleanup.

      Cancellation emits one cancelled terminal event with COMPILER_CANCELLED when
      transport remains writable, returns no report, and leaves the latest completed
      report unchanged.

      Caller cancellation before durable commit begins terminates delegated work and
      returns COMPILER_CANCELLED. Cancellation after commit begins is ignored; commit
      success or failure and terminal-event delivery determine settlement.

      Successful evaluation releases access, commits the complete SessionRecord,
      releases the lease, and only then emits completed and returns the same report.

      Failure writing the required completed terminal event after commit rejects with
      COMPILER_FAILED and returns no report; the committed session state remains
      durable for the next operation.

      Evaluate commit failure emits no completed event, returns no report, rejects
      with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE, and terminates the session through
      verified cleanup. A later evaluate cannot resume that identity.

      Refresh commit failure emits no CompilationEvent, rejects with
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE, and terminates the session through verified
      cleanup.

      Process exit after proposal materialization but before SessionRecord commit
      leaves the preceding record durable. The next opener detects the advanced
      materialization, rejects with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE, attempts verified
      cleanup while holding its lease, and abandons the session; unverifiable
      ownership preserves evidence for manual recovery.

      Evaluate establishes its event stream before session access. Establishment
      failure returns COMPILER_FAILED; later proposal and workspace rejection emits
      one failed event carrying its exact stable code, while terminal-write failure
      supersedes it with COMPILER_FAILED.

      An operation accepted immediately before expiresAt may finish and establishes a
      new deadline when it settles. At exactly expiresAt, expiry wins before a new
      operation can acquire the lease.

      After successful expiry cleanup, evaluate and refresh reject with
      COMPILER_SESSION_EXPIRED and close succeeds idempotently. Expiry cleanup
      failure enters cleanup-failed and only close may retry.

      Cleanup failure prevents later evaluation. A successful close retry completes
      cleanup and leaves the session closed; evaluation requires a new session.

      Unverifiable workspace ownership requires manual directory handling and session
      abandonment before a new session is created. The persisted session enters
      manual-recovery-required; evaluate, refresh, close, and automatic deletion all
      reject without touching proposal contents, while read-only status remains
      available.

      A green proposal report still requires ReviewGate approval before repository
      mutation.
    }
  }
}
